From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 21:43:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08075/11362] [outer-loop] build_window_from_labels: induction
 windows without a GameAdapter

From an operator question asked three times: what is the purpose of a GameAdapter
that only solves one level? Measured answer: for 18 of the 25 public games,
almost none.

Those adapters' action_labels return a SINGLE forced label at each step, replaying
a plan that was already banked. Everything solve_adaptered actually provides --
verifier-routed search, hazard pruning, learned-verifier warm start, state dedup
-- is unexercised when the action set has one element; the search is a straight
line through a known solution. They were not solving L1. They were satisfying a
function signature, because build_window obtained its labels via solve_adaptered,
which requires an adapter.

arc_solver_kit.reproduce() already showed the coupling was unnecessary: it takes
(labels, apply) directly, and was used earlier today to gate-verify wa30's whole
670-action L9 route with NO adapter at all. So the adapter requirement was an
artifact of build_window's implementation, not a conceptual need.

build_window is now a thin caller responsible only for OBTAINING labels (by
search, through the adapter); replay and window-cutting live in
build_window_from_labels(game, labels, apply, *, k, warmup_label,
label_to_action_data). A game with a banked route can build a corpus window
directly, and GameAdapter can go back to meaning one thing: "this game has a
searchable action space".

Behaviour preserved -- the point of test_label_path_matches_adapter_path, which
asserts both routes yield byte-identical transitions for wa30 (12 window / 33
full / cell 1). If they diverged, a corpus built one way would not be the corpus
built the other and every A/B through either becomes incomparable.

Two things the tests had to work around, both worth recording:
- "C:1" cannot be applied by _default_json_apply at all (it json.loads the
  label), so the parse-guard tests use a lenient stand-in apply to reach the code
  under test. My first version tested the wrong failure.
- Building both windows costs ~520 MB, and the repo's memory watchdog attributes
  setup->teardown growth to the test. Computed at COLLECTION time instead,
  following the fix test_arc_live_generator_pin.py documents for its own 590 MB
  import.

10 tests. No other caller affected: build_window's signature is unchanged and
build_progress_window still goes through it.
